@title Erc1967Proxy
 @notice Lab 14 helper — minimal ERC-1967 proxy for the UUPS labs.
 @dev Implementation address lives at the EIP-1967 slot. `initData` runs
      atomically inside the creation transaction and must be nonempty.

@title SafeAccess
 @notice Lab 02 — Remediated access control using msg.sender.
 @dev The fix is one keyword: replace `tx.origin` with `msg.sender`.
 Why it works:
   msg.sender is always the immediate caller. When the owner EOA
   calls a malicious contract that tries to chain-call SafeAccess,
   msg.sender will be the malicious contract's address — not the
   owner's. The authorization check fails.

@notice Swap with an explicit minimum output and deadline.
 @param minAmountOut Reverts if execution yields less than this amount.
 @param deadline Reverts if the transaction lands after this timestamp.

@title SafeAmm
 @notice Lab 06 — enforces caller-specified minimum output and deadlines.
 @dev These bounds can reject execution below the caller's minimum; they do
      not prevent every profitable sandwich. This educational AMM accepts
      optional-return ERC-20s with exact, non-rebasing balance changes only.

@title SafeFotVault
 @notice Lab 13 — credits deposits from the token balance delta.
 @dev Incoming transfer fees are supported when the token reports balances
      honestly. Rebases and transfers that debit the vault by more than the
      requested withdrawal are unsupported. This is an educational model.

@notice Owner-only recovery of ETH never credited as collateral.
 @dev Collateral is never rescuable. Nested calls recompute the live
      surplus after each transfer, so no reentrancy guard is needed.

@title SafeLending
 @notice Lab 04 — Rejects unfunded collateral, cumulative over-borrowing,
         and zero, future, stale, or below-minimum oracle prices.
 @dev Educational debt-accounting model: no loan asset is transferred.
      Debt and normalized prices have 18 decimals. For L2 or TWAP checks,
      use ConfiguredSafeLending with feeds appropriate to the deployment.
      Owner-only rescueETH recovers only unaccounted ETH; user collateral
      can never be rescued. Each rescue uses the live balance, so nested
      rescues cannot over-drain and need no reentrancy guard.

@title SafeMint
 @notice Lab 09 — Role-gated minting, a lifetime issuance cap, and ETH
         backing for all outstanding 1:1 redeemable claims.
 @dev Assumes one ETH of reserve backs each supply unit. The admin controls
      minters; issuance has a lifetime cap and outstanding supply cannot
      exceed current ETH backing. This is an educational model, not a
      production token or a proof that admin/minter keys are secure.

@title SafePool
 @notice Lab 05 — rejects zero-share deposits and seeds fixed dead shares.
 @dev Dead shares increase the donation needed for the tested first-deposit
      attack, but do not cap the first depositor's ownership when that
      depositor contributes much more than DEAD_SHARES. This model does not
      guarantee every donation strategy is unprofitable.

@title SafeProxy
 @notice Lab 07 — Remediated: EIP-1967 dedicated storage slots.
 @dev Implementation and admin addresses live at keccak256-derived
      slots no sane storage layout can reach, so implementation state
      and proxy administration can never collide.
      Admin-only rescueETH recovers proxy-held ETH. The admin can already
      upgrade to an arbitrary implementation, so rescue adds no new trust
      assumption. The proxy's own functions take precedence over fallback
      dispatch to the implementation.

@title SafeImplV1
 @notice Initial implementation. `value` occupies slot 0 — harmless,
         because the proxy no longer keeps anything there.

@title SafeImplV2
 @notice Upgraded implementation. Appends a new variable; existing
         slots keep their meaning (append-only storage layout).

@title SafeRouter
 @notice Lab 10 — Owner-managed, backed allocations with guarded claims
         consumed before transfer; failed transfers roll back accounting.
 @dev Assumes a trusted, exact-transfer, bool-returning token with honest
      balance reporting. Fee-on-transfer and rebasing tokens are unsupported.

@title SafeSignature
 @notice Lab 08 — Remediated: EIP-712 typed signatures bound to this
         contract and chain, with per-recipient nonces, deadlines and a
         high-s malleability guard.
 @dev A signature can only ever authorize one payout: the nonce is
      consumed before the transfer, the domain separator pins the digest
      to (name, version, chainId, address(this)), and an expired deadline
      or a malleable high-s value reverts.

@title SafeSplitter
 @notice Lab 12 — Remediates recipient-blocked payouts with pull payments.
 @dev Batches only account for ETH; each payee claims independently. This
      assumes payees can receive ETH within 30,000 gas or use withdrawTo.

@title SafeSwap
 @notice Lab 03 — Remediated: uses a cumulative-price TWAP instead of spot reserves.
 @dev The cumulative price advances with elapsed time, so a same-block spot
      manipulation contributes zero weight to the TWAP. A consumer
      must have at least PERIOD of observation history before reading it.

@title SafeUupsVault
 @notice Lab 14 — Remediated: one-shot initializer + implementation lock.
 @dev `initialize` is guarded by an explicit `initialized` flag, and the
      constructor sets it on the implementation itself so nobody can ever
      initialize the raw implementation's storage. Combined with an atomic
      deploy-plus-initialize proxy constructor call, no takeover window
      exists — not even a one-block one.

@title SafeVault
 @notice Lab 01 — Remediated: applies checks-effects-interactions.
 @dev The balance is decremented BEFORE the external call. A malicious
      receiver that tries to re-enter withdraw() will fail the balance
      check on the second call.

@title SafeVault4626
 @notice Lab 11 — virtual-liquidity share accounting with zero-share rejection.
 @dev Equal virtual asset/share offsets make the empty-vault conversion 1:1;
      they do not provide a decimal precision offset or prevent every
      donation attack. Deposits that would mint zero shares revert. This
      model supports exact-transfer, non-rebasing assets only.

@notice Vulnerability: no `minAmountOut`, no deadline. The caller
         accepts whatever price is live when the transaction lands,
         including a price an attacker just moved in the same block.

@title VulnerableAmm
 @notice Lab 06 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. `swap()` executes at whatever price is live at the
      moment of execution and accepts no slippage bound or deadline, so
      any transaction can be sandwiched for profit.

@title VulnerableFotVault
 @notice Lab 13 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. The vault credits the *requested* deposit amount while
      a fee-on-transfer token delivers less, so every deposit mints claims
      that exceed the value actually received. The shortfall is silently
      socialized onto honest depositors, and a cycling attacker extracts it.

@title VulnerableMint
 @notice Lab 09 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. A token fully backed 1:1 by ETH in this contract,
      whose `mint` entry point was meant for the team — but the `admin`
      state variable is tracked and never checked. Anyone can mint
      themselves tokens and immediately redeem them for the reserve.

@notice Donate ETH without receiving shares — models any value
         entering the pool outside `deposit` (donation, selfdestruct,
         a misrouted transfer, ...).

@title VulnerablePool
 @notice Lab 05 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. Share minting uses floor division without any
      inflation protection. An attacker who deposits first can inflate
      `totalAssets` with a donation so the next depositor mints zero
      shares, then redeem a single share for the entire pool balance.

@title VulnerableProxy
 @notice Lab 07 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. The proxy stores `admin` and `implementation` in
      regular storage slots 0 and 1 — the same slots the implementation
      contract uses for its own state. Every state write performed
      through the proxy clobbers proxy administration.

@title VulnerableImplV1
 @notice Naive implementation whose `value` sits in slot 0.
 @dev `value` and the proxy's `admin` are the SAME storage slot when
      called through the proxy. `setValue` rewrites the admin.

@title VulnerableRouter
 @notice Lab 10 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. An airdrop-style claims router that calls
      `token.transfer` and ignores the returned `bool`. Tokens that
      signal failure by returning false (USDT-style) instead of
      reverting make the router record a claim that never paid out —
      the user's entitlement is erased and the tokens stay in the
      router forever.

@title VulnerableSignature
 @notice Lab 08 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. Withdrawals are authorized with a raw ECDSA
      signature over `keccak256(abi.encodePacked(recipient, amount))`.
      The signed message carries no nonce, no deadline, no chain id and
      no verifying-contract address, and consumed signatures are never
      tracked. Anyone who observes one valid payout (in the mempool or
      on-chain) can resubmit the exact same signature until the vault is
      empty, and can also reuse the signature verbatim on any other
      contract that hashes the same layout (cross-contract replay).

@title VulnerableSplitter
 @notice Lab 12 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. `distribute()` walks the entire payee list in one
      transaction and anyone can append payees, so the gas cost of a payout
      round is attacker-controlled. Once `payees.length` passes the block
      gas limit divided by the per-payee cost, `distribute()` reverts
      out-of-gas for everyone, forever — revenue is frozen with no
      alternative exit path.

@title VulnerableUupsVault
 @notice Lab 14 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. `initialize()` carries no one-shot guard, and the
      implementation's constructor does not lock its own storage. A proxy
      deployed now and initialized later — or a deployment transaction
      frontrun — hands ownership to whoever calls `initialize` first, and
      owning a UUPS implementation means `upgradeToAndCall` to arbitrary
      logic, i.e. total loss of custody.

@title VulnerableVault
 @notice Lab 01 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. The withdraw function sends ETH before updating
      accounting, allowing a malicious receiver to re-enter.

@title VulnerableVault4626
 @notice Lab 11 — Intentionally vulnerable educational example.
 @dev DO NOT DEPLOY. Share pricing floors in the depositor's disfavor and
      `totalAssets` is read straight from the token balance, so a direct
      donation can inflate the share price. The first depositor can weaponize
      the degenerate zero/low-supply regime: donate, wait for the next
      depositor to round to zero shares, and redeem the whole vault for
      essentially one share.
